"Rewrite $8{x}^{2}-4x$ by completing the square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184804 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62243cf5-4bf2-5f4d-9d71-de0fec8db876" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184804 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, we take out a factor of $8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n8({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x) & =8({(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184993 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184993 #edb/ref 1061 37365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184993 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10a00ef6-4086-5bc9-b1f4-a84242b5b363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184993 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184995 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184995 #edb/ref 1061 37390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184995 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65a066ee-807a-50d9-8957-f31276799bef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184995 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184996 #edb/ref 1061 37395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184996 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7