76 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271859 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271859 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186270843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271859 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c0bb4535-ddbc-5c9e-b8f1-ec33a353b4ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272054 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272054 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140321" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272054 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272054 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}+10y+20)$ by completing the square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272054 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3fe4be51-7638-5af1-be56-d66a24129d70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272054 #edb/ref 1074 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+10y+20\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 5y+20\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $20$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-{5}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+5)^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}+5y+20) & =-({(y+5)}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-{(y+5)}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =5-(y+5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272059 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272059 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272059 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272059 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-x-2)$ by completing the square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272059 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "793139fd-bc0b-52b9-b28f-1f9a809139b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272059 #edb/ref 1074 "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x-2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-x-2) & =-({(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272081 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272081 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140410" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272081 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272081 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+5y-5$ by completing the square." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272081 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "afc8b4da-a3f1-5590-a7d5-e7a094b395bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272081 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-5y+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-5y+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we 